# systemd unit for the vike-tradehub headless trading DAEMON (headless two-layer plan, Layer 2,
# PR-9), in the ONE-PROJECT-FOLDER shape.
#
# ⚠ EVERY PATH BELOW IS THE PLACEHOLDER PROJECT ROOT /srv/vike-<unit>. It is an example, not a
# requirement — the daemon works from ANY absolute path, and the CI box uses /var/lib/vike,
# which it SHARES with the tape recorder (one folder, two daemons, each with its own write grant).
# Choose your own root by ONE rule: a single directory on a disk with room, owned by the user the
# service runs as, holding bin/ settings/ (with state/ and state/logs/) and data/. Then substitute
# it everywhere in the installed copy and check nothing was missed:
#
#   R=/your/project/root
#   sed "s,/srv/vike-<unit>,$R,g" deploy/vike-tradehub.service > /tmp/vike-tradehub.service
#   grep -n /srv/vike-<unit> /tmp/vike-tradehub.service    # must print NOTHING
#   sudo install -Dm644 /tmp/vike-tradehub.service /etc/systemd/system/vike-tradehub.service
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now vike-tradehub
#
# For a --user install put it in ~/.config/systemd/user/ and drop the sudo/`User=`; read the
# hardening block at the bottom first, because under a --user manager the sandbox directives are
# applied as NO-OPS on a stock Ubuntu box (measured — docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md).
#
# ⚠ /opt/vike is GONE. This unit used to install a binary into /opt/vike, its log into
# /var/log/vike-tradehub and its state into a third place, and the runbook walked an operator into
# `install -d /opt/vike/settings`. Three locations meant three things to grant, three to back up and
# three to get wrong — and the settings walk, the log destination and the HALT sentinel default all
# key off the PROJECT ROOT, so splitting them bought nothing and cost a class of "the daemon started
# with no settings at all" bugs. One folder, like Freqtrade and OctoBot: nothing in /opt, nothing in
# /var/log. See docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md.
#
# ⚠⚠ **ONE UNIT PER DAEMON, AND THIS IS THE TRADING DAEMON'S.** Until 2026-09-16 this directory
# also carried `deploy/vike-tradehub-project.service` — the same shape with the CI box's real root
# filled in, installed under THIS file's name. The owner ruled that shape out: a daemon has ONE
# unit file, named without a suffix, and the project root is a substitutable parameter rather than
# a second file. The `sed` recipe above is what produced the second file, so a tracked copy of its
# output was a second spelling of one fact.
#
# ⚠⚠ **WHAT PROD2 SUBSTITUTES, AND WHAT IT ADDS AND OMITS.** Kept here because the file that
# carried it is gone and because every line of it is a decision somebody made once. MEASURED
# 2026-09-16. `docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md` is the operator authority for that box.
#
#   the root        `/srv/vike-<unit>` -> the box's own project folder, SHARED with the data
#                   daemon. One folder, two daemons, each with its own write grant.
#   User=/Group=    ADDED. Deliberately NOT shipped here: this file is published to the public
#                   source mirror, whose redaction rewrites a box's user name to a phrase that is
#                   not a legal systemd user name — so a `User=` line here would publish a unit
#                   nobody can install. Add yours in the installed copy.
#   WantedBy=       `default.target` -> `multi-user.target`. This file ships the `--user`-friendly
#                   spelling; a SYSTEM install wants `multi-user.target`, and the CI box has run both
#                   daemons as SYSTEM units since 2026-08-17 (which is when this file's sandbox
#                   directives stopped being no-ops there).
#   OnFailure=      ADDED inline, as `OnFailure=vike-notify@%n.service`. This file instead points
#                   at `deploy/attach-onfailure.sh`, which attaches the same hook as a DROP-IN.
#                   ⚠ Do not do both — `OnFailure=` is a LIST directive and the notifier would
#                   fire twice. Pick the inline line or the drop-in, per box.
#   ExecStartPre=   OMITTED there, and this file KEEPS it. The omission is a declared partial, not
#                   an oversight: adding the pre-flight ARMS a new refusal on the next redeploy of
#                   a daemon that has been up for days, and that is an operator act to sequence
#                   rather than a side effect of copying a file. A box that wants the refusal adds
#                   the line and restarts once, deliberately, having first run
#                   `bin/vike-cli config check` by hand.
#   VIKE_HALT_FILE= OMITTED there, and this file KEEPS it. Omitting it is what exercises rung 2 of
#                   `crates/vike-bridge-core/src/halt.rs`'s path ladder — the built-in
#                   `<project>/settings/state/HALT` default — on a real box. ⚠ That rung only
#                   lands inside the write grant below because the daemon DECLARES its state
#                   directory from the settings directory it resolved; a box that omits the line
#                   AND repoints `VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=` moves its kill switch with it.
#
# ⚠⚠ **AND THAT DEPLOYMENT IS LIVE, NOT PAPER.** Kept from the deleted file because it is the one
# claim about that box a reader most needs and the one that rotted fastest. MEASURED read-only
# 2026-08-16 on a daemon up 7 days (pre-migration, hence --user; after the SYSTEM switch the same
# question is `journalctl -u vike-tradehub` and the answer was the same string):
#   journalctl --user -u vike-tradehub | grep '"kind":"ready"'  ->  "mode":"LIVE (venue=bybit)"
#   tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/<MainPID>/environ                      ->  VIKE_TRADEHUB_LIVE=1
#   <root>/settings/secrets.env  (key NAMES only)               ->  BYBIT_DEMO_API_KEY/_API_SECRET
#   <root>/settings/policy.toml                                 ->  max_notional_per_order = 100
# The venue is bybit's DEMO account, so no mainnet money is at stake — but a REAL venue adapter is
# mounted, orders go on a real wire, and `vike_run::build_paper_maker_core` is not what runs.
#
# ⚠ THE BANNER ABOVE IS A TRANSCRIPT, AND ITS `venue=` NO LONGER MEANS WHAT IT MEANT. That string
# was rendered from the PROFILE's mount set; today it names the set that actually ARMED
# (`build_node`'s own `live_venues` record — one entry per venue that got a real
# `ExecutionClient`), which the CREDENTIAL STORE decides rather than tradehub.toml. A later
# measurement on that box logged NINE venues in that record under this same one-venue banner, which
# is what got it changed. So do not diff a today's banner against the line above and read the
# difference as a redeploy — re-measure, and see docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md's "What `mode` names".
# **`LIVE (venue=none)` is a valid answer there: gate on, nothing armed.**
#
# ⚠ NONE OF THAT IS CONFIGURED IN THIS FILE, which is why the claim rotted unnoticed for a week.
# The escalation arrives through `EnvironmentFile=-<root>/.env` below — and the credentials sit in
# the settings tree beside it. Every escalation is still a separate explicit gate, each documented
# in the runbook; the lesson is that **a unit cannot state its own paper-vs-live mode**, because a
# one-line edit to a file it merely REFERENCES flips it with nothing here changing. RE-MEASURED
# 2026-09-16, key NAMES only (`cut -d= -f1 <root>/.env`): that file holds POLY_PROXY_ENABLED and
# VIKE_RUN_PROFILE and nothing else — there is no VIKE_TRADEHUB_LIVE line, because the live master
# gate comes from the FILE layer now (`grep -n tradehub_live <root>/settings/flags.toml` answers
# `tradehub_live = true`). That is the settings-unification work reaching the deployment, not a
# change of posture.
#
# ⚠ AND ONE COMMENT INSIDE THAT `.env` IS FALSE, which matters more than the drift above because it
# is what an operator reaching for the brake would read. Above VIKE_RUN_PROFILE it says *"ARMS the
# tradehub's live venue mount. Removing this line puts it back to paper."* Removing it does NOT put
# that box back to paper: with the live gate on and no run profile, `vike_mount`'s
# `require_live_risk_budget` refuses the mount pre-connect (`MountError::MissingRiskBudget`) and the
# daemon EXITS FAILURE. The paper brake is `tradehub_live = false` in flags.toml.
#
# Ask the running binary: the ready banner's `"mode"` is the authority, and
# docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md's "confirm what is actually deployed" section is the procedure.
#
# The one-folder shape is MACHINE-CHECKED: crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs reads every
# unit in this directory and fails if one names a second location, spreads its write grant over more
# than one ReadWritePaths= LINE, grants a path that covers a binary it runs or its own settings
# directory, sets VIKE_LOG_DIR, points EnvironmentFile= at the credential store, or runs an
# ExecStartPre= binary from outside the project folder (or behind a `-`, which would make the
# pre-check non-fatal).
#
# PAPER variant: this mounts the A-S SpreadMaker on the PRODUCTION live core over the PAPER exchange
# (vike_run::build_paper_maker_core) — ZERO real-money / credential / geo risk. It has NO network
# control surface (that is PR-10/11/12) and no live feed (real-venue mounting is the PR-8 follow-up):
# PR-9 proves the daemon LIFECYCLE runs headless and survives an SSH disconnect (R7).

[Unit]
Description=vike-tradehub — headless trading daemon (A-S maker; paper-vs-live comes from <project>/.env)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple

# The daemon reads its whole config from ONE reviewable TOML profile (LEAN "environments", S8).
#
# ⚠ **THE RELEASE SHIPS ONE BACKEND BINARY, `vike-backend`, AND `tradehub` IS A TOOL INSIDE IT.**
# That is why ExecStart below reads `bin/vike-backend trade` rather than `bin/vike-tradehub`:
# since 2026-09-09 the release attaches the multicall and `vike-cli` and nothing else, because the
# multicall already contained every per-tool binary and shipping both shipped the same program twice
# (629 MB against 148 MB, measured on v0.1.20). Dispatch is on a SUBCOMMAND, so there is no symlink
# here and no `203/EXEC` class to inherit from a dangling one.
# ⚠ THE VERB LOST ITS `vike-` PREFIX WHEN THE BINARY WAS RENAMED `vike` -> `vike-backend`, and the
# binary and the verb are ONE EDIT, never two. `crates/vike/src/lib.rs`'s `resolve` matches a tool
# by EXACT string with no aliases, so `bin/vike-backend vike-tradehub` and `bin/vike tradehub` are
# each `Route::Usage(2)`: exit 2 with a tool list on stderr — a trading daemon that "started" and
# quietly did nothing, which is a worse outcome than a missing binary. Until the rename this line
# said the opposite (`vike tradehub` printed the dispatcher's help and exited, MEASURED on the
# released v0.1.20 binary, so the tool name WAS the full binary name); that measurement is kept as
# the evidence for why one half cannot be edited alone, not as today's spelling.
# Building it yourself is the same one command it always was, under the multicall's own package —
# and the `--bin` follows the PACKAGE name, because crates/vike/Cargo.toml declares no `[[bin]]`:
#   cargo build --release -p vike-backend --bin vike-backend --features full
# ⚠ **THE TELEGRAM CONTROL CHANNEL IS COMPILED IN, AND THIS BLOCK SAID THE OPPOSITE UNTIL
# 2026-09-09.** It read "this DEFAULT build contains NO Telegram control channel", which was true of
# `cargo build -p vike-tradehub` with no features and is NOT true of what an operator installs: the
# release builds the daemon `--features …,vike-tradehub/telegram,…` and the multicall's `full`
# resolves to the same set (`crates/vike-ops/tests/multicall_gate.rs` holds the two equal), so a
# remote-chat order path IS linked into the binary this unit starts. `docs/decisions/0035-…` is
# where that call was made: a production box must run the binary users get.
# What keeps it inert is therefore RUNTIME, not linkage, and all four gates must be true together:
# VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL=1 + VIKE_TELEGRAM_CONTROL=1 + VIKE_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN + a non-empty
# VIKE_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_CHAT_IDS. None is set here, and `Environment=` lines in this file are the
# whole of what this unit passes. ⚠ A stale VIKE_TELEGRAM_* line in an INHERITED environment used
# to be harmless because nothing could read it; now it is one of four, so treat it as live
# configuration. Building the tool without the feature is still possible and is now the only way
# back to "cannot arm at all":
#   cargo build --release -p vike-tradehub --bin vike-tradehub   # no `telegram`, no multicall
# ⚠ That allowlist is per-CHAT, and a chat is not a person: an allowlisted GROUP authorizes every
# member, including everyone anyone later adds, with no config change. Every accepted command names
# its sender in the audit trail; to also RESTRICT by sender, set the optional
# VIKE_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USER_IDS (comma-separated numeric ids, ANDed with the chat list).
#
# WorkingDirectory IS the project root, and that is what makes the rest of this file short: the
# settings walk, the rolling log and the HALT sentinel default all resolve off it with no extra
# variable (crates/vike-model/src/state_path.rs's `project_state_dir`,
# crates/vike-bridge-core/src/halt.rs's `resolve_halt_path`). ⚠ Where Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR
# is set (it is, below), THAT names the project instead and the walk never runs — for the sentinel
# too, since the daemon hands its one resolved settings directory to
# crates/vike-bridge-core/src/halt.rs's `declare_project_state_dir`. The two agree here by
# construction; they would not if a WorkingDirectory= were ever changed without the other.
#
# ⚠ THE UPGRADE IS TWO ACTS AND NOTHING SEQUENCES THEM. `deploy/sbin/vike-trader-ci-deploy` replaces
# the BINARY, unattended, on a tag; this FILE only reaches the box when an operator runs the
# `install -Dm644` recipe at the top and `systemctl daemon-reload`. For one release the helper
# installed `bin/vike-backend` BESIDE `bin/vike` rather than over it, so an un-flipped unit kept
# starting instead of dying `203/EXEC` unattended — at the OLD version, which the helper's `status`
# and its deploy-time SKEW warning both named — while this line was flipped, daemon-reloaded and
# restarted. That cycle closed on v0.1.23: `bin/vike` is a retired name the next deploy unlinks, so
# a unit still naming it is `203/EXEC` at its next restart, and the SKEW warning stays for the next
# time this line and the helper disagree.
WorkingDirectory=/srv/vike-<unit>
ExecStart=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-backend trade --config /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/tradehub.toml

# --- PRE-FLIGHT: refuse a broken configuration instead of starting degraded -----------------------
# Without this line the daemon STARTS ANYWAY when its configuration is broken, and every way it can
# be broken is silent by construction: a `settings/` that was never created makes the project walk
# answer nothing, a `secrets.env` that has become unreadable logs one line and returns an EMPTY map,
# and both end in the same place — every venue on paper, nothing in the log that says why, and a
# `systemctl status` that reads healthy. The library layer had already drawn the distinction that
# matters (crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `SecretsError` exists precisely so that "not
# configured" and "cannot open" never look the same); nothing at DEPLOY time acted on it, which is
# the gap this line closes. docs/decisions/0013-degrade-vs-refuse.md is the rule it implements.
#
# `vike-cli config check` resolves the SAME settings directory through the SAME loader this daemon
# uses (crates/vike-config/src/load.rs's `load`, via `describe`) and makes the EXIT CODE the product.
# A non-zero status fails the unit BEFORE ExecStart runs, so the daemon never reaches a venue with a
# configuration nobody validated.
#
# ⚠ It refuses only what a silent difference would MISREPRESENT: a settings file that does not parse
# or carries an unknown/removed key, a REMOVED environment variable (which the EnvironmentFile= below
# can still deliver), and a VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR naming a directory that is not there. An ABSENT
# credential store is NOT a failure — absent credentials ARE the live gate, this unit ships as a
# PAPER daemon whose store is deliberately empty, and a pre-check that refused one would make a
# correct fresh install unstartable.
#
# ⚠ An UNREADABLE credential store — one that exists and cannot be opened — is the case this check
# was written for, and its disposition is COMPUTED, not fixed. On a paper box it is a WARNING and
# this daemon still starts: docs/decisions/0013-degrade-vs-refuse.md classifies the daemon's own
# behaviour there (one log line, an empty map, every venue paper) as a conforming degrade, and a
# pre-check that stopped a working paper deployment over a file it never opens would take down more
# than it protects. It becomes a REFUSAL when the process environment ARMS a live venue
# (crates/vike-config/src/arming.rs's `CREDENTIAL_FILE_ARMING_REFUSED` is the table): then the
# operator has asked for real money and the process cannot read the file that venue authenticates
# from, so it would place no orders while every surface reads live. Run
# `vike-cli config check --strict` if you want the paper case counted too.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT `--strict`. That flag promotes every WARNING to a failure, including a 0644
# `secrets.env` — and crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `PermissionWarning` argues that one must
# never be a refusal, because refusing strands somebody mid-setup with every venue on paper, which
# is worse than the exposure it objects to. Run `vike-cli config check --strict` by hand when
# auditing a box; never from a unit.
#
# ⚠ One `--strict` surprise worth knowing before you run it: an ABSENT credential store beside the
# `<project>/.env` below is a WARNING (crates/vike-secrets/src/store.rs's `LegacyStoreWarning` — a
# pre-one-store leftover and a systemd EnvironmentFile are the same file to anything that does not
# read it), so `--strict` calls that pair an error. docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md step 3 avoids it by
# creating an empty `settings/secrets.env`; a box that skipped that step will see it. The default
# disposition — what the line above runs — passes either way.
#
# ⚠ NO leading `-` on this line. That prefix makes a failed ExecStartPre non-fatal, which is the
# same as deleting the line while appearing to have it.
#
# ⚠ IT NEEDS A SECOND BINARY IN bin/. `vike-cli` is the command-line surface, not the daemon, so it
# must be installed beside it or systemd fails the unit with status=203/EXEC — a loud failure, but
# not the one you were debugging. Build and install it with the daemon (it is DataFusion-free and
# transport-free, so it costs a small binary and no runtime dependency):
#   cargo build --release -p vike-cli --bin vike-cli
#   install -m755 target/release/vike-cli /srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli
ExecStartPre=/srv/vike-<unit>/bin/vike-cli config check

# --- stop ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `systemctl stop` IS a graceful stop. SIGTERM raises the daemon's stop flag — the SAME flag the
# interactive `shutdown`/`quit` word raises — and the ordinary teardown runs: the resting-order
# cancel sweep (when `cancel_orders_on_shutdown` is on), the strategy-state save, the terminal
# journal snapshot, the feed quiesce, and the bounded core join. One stop flag, many triggers; the
# decision, the the CI box measurements and the options that were refused are in docs/ops/graceful-stop.md.
#
# ⚠ Before 2026-08-07 this was NOT true, and a box still running an older binary is unchanged: the
# headless path ended in a park loop SIGTERM could not interrupt, so `systemctl stop` ran no Rust at
# all, `cancel_orders_on_shutdown` was silently bypassed, and the book was ABANDONED at the venue
# while `systemctl status` reported success. Check `journalctl -u vike-tradehub` for
# `SIGTERM/SIGINT will stop this daemon gracefully` at startup — a build without it needs
# docs/ops/kill-switches.md section C (HALT, then market-exit) before every stop.
#
# Under systemd stdin is /dev/null, so the stdio words are still unreachable here and a non-tty EOF
# deliberately does NOT stop the daemon (it would exit at startup). The periodic one-line JSON
# summaries + acks land in the journal (StandardOutput defaults to `journal`); read them with
# `journalctl -u vike-tradehub -f`. For interactive control (type `shutdown`/`quit`, or a JSON
# Command), run the binary directly in a terminal — stdin is then a TTY.
#
# There is deliberately NO ExecStop=: the daemon stops on the signal, so a stop command could only
# add a SECOND stop route to a design whose whole invariant is that there is one.
KillSignal=SIGTERM
# ⚠ MUST EXCEED the profile's `[daemon] shutdown_deadline_ms`, or SIGKILL wins the race and the
# teardown is cut in half — which is worse than the old ungraceful stop, because it interrupts the
# cancel sweep at an arbitrary point instead of never starting it. The daemon's own teardown is
# hard-capped at that profile deadline (vike_ops::shutdown::run_with_deadline), and
# `the_default_shutdown_deadline_fits_inside_the_units_stop_timeout` in crates/vike-tradehub/src/main.rs
# checks THIS line against the shipped default. Raise the profile deadline and raise this too.
TimeoutStopSec=10

# --- the OUT-OF-BAND kill switch (HALT sentinel) --------------------------------------------------
# The one stop that works when the runtime is wedged: the venue adapters check this file's existence
# at the submit/modify boundary, so `touch` it and the very NEXT order is refused — no IPC, no event
# loop, no responsive control channel needed. `rm` resumes. See docs/ops/kill-switches.md §C.
#
#   touch /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/HALT     # stop new orders
#   rm    /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/HALT     # resume
#
# (settings/ is mode 700 owned by the service user, so run those AS that user — `sudo -u <user>` on a
# system install, your own shell on a --user one.)
#
# ⚠ This line used to be the ONLY thing holding the switch up, and it is still worth keeping. The
# sentinel's built-in default WAS `<exe_dir>/HALT`, and ProtectSystem=strict (below) mounts the whole
# filesystem read-only — so on the old three-location layout the default path returned EROFS
# (measured, under a transient unit with these exact directives) and the kill switch could not be
# armed at all. The default now resolves to <project>/settings/state/HALT by itself, i.e. the very
# file this line names, so a unit that forgets it is no longer silently disarmed. Keeping the line
# makes the answer EXPLICIT and independent of WorkingDirectory — exactly like VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR
# below — and it is the fix that needs no rebuild on a box still running an older binary.
# (the CI box's installed unit deliberately OMITS it, to exercise the default rather than paper over
# it — see the WHAT PROD2 SUBSTITUTES block in this header. Both positions are defensible; what is
# not defensible is a THIRD path.)
# Never widen the sandbox instead: see the ReadWritePaths block at the bottom for why the exe
# directory must stay read-only.
#
# ⚠ A BLANK value (`Environment=VIKE_HALT_FILE=`) is ignored rather than honoured. It would otherwise
# resolve the sentinel to a path that can neither exist nor be created — the same unarmed switch,
# wearing a different hat.
#
# The value must name a path under ReadWritePaths= whose PARENT exists — nothing creates it lazily,
# and an operator discovers a wrong value at the worst possible moment. <project>/settings/state is
# both granted below and mandatory for the unit to start at all, which is what makes it the right
# anchor. The daemon also says which path it resolved, once, when the first real venue mounts
# (`HALT sentinel path resolved`, or `HALT KILL SWITCH IS NOT ARMABLE` at error) — but verify by hand
# after any deployment change anyway, while nothing is on fire:
#   touch /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/HALT && rm /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/HALT
Environment=VIKE_HALT_FILE=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/HALT

# --- turn the trace FILE log DOWN ----------------------------------------------------------------
# RUST_LOG/VIKE_LOG filter the CONSOLE only; the file layer defaults to `trace`. A long headless run
# would otherwise grow a colossal JSON file no console setting can turn down — turn it down here.
Environment=VIKE_LOG_FILE_LEVEL=warn
# ⚠ Deliberately NO VIKE_LOG_DIR. Unset, the project layer resolves the rolling log to
# <project>/settings/state/logs — INSIDE the single write grant at the bottom of this file. Naming a
# directory here would put the log somewhere that grant does not cover, and under a manager where the
# sandbox is actually enforced the daemon then cannot write its own log. Watch it with
# `du -sh /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/logs`.
# Optional: the mount honors VIKE_JOURNAL_DIR (write-ahead command journal, read INSIDE
# build_paper_maker_core). Keep it inside the project root so the one grant still covers it:
# Environment=VIKE_JOURNAL_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state/journal

# --- OPT-IN ESCALATIONS (default OFF; each is a separate explicit gate) ---------------------------
# ⚠ Read docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md before enabling ANY of these. Each line is commented out on
# purpose — the shipped unit is the zero-risk PAPER daemon with no network surface.
#
# LIVE trading: swap the paper mount for the real 12-venue build_node core. From here the daemon is
# bound by the live-gate rules in CLAUDE.md — a venue trades ONLY if its creds are in the credential
# store (NOT the .env below; see the block after next), Aster is MAINNET-ONLY (exclude ASTER_* from
# validation), Polymarket must egress via arbdub.
# Environment=VIKE_TRADEHUB_LIVE=1
#
# Observe/control server (localhost only; reach it over `ssh -L 7879:localhost:7879 <box>`). Observe
# = read-only; Control additionally admits order commands and is DOUBLE-GATED (key + the CONTROL
# flag) and server-edge notional/rate-limited. Keep the two keys SEPARATE + separately revocable.
#
# ⚠ "localhost only" is ENFORCED, not advisory: the handshake is plaintext and authenticates the
# connection rather than each frame, so the tunnel IS the confidentiality/integrity layer. A
# non-loopback address (0.0.0.0 and :: included) is refused at startup — the daemon logs the reason
# and keeps trading headless — unless flags.toml's `tradehub_allow_public_bind` (or
# VIKE_TRADEHUB_ALLOW_PUBLIC_BIND=1) says the listening network is trusted.
# Environment=VIKE_TRADEHUB_ADDR=127.0.0.1:7879
# Environment=VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL=1
# Environment=VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL_RATE=20
# (VIKE_TRADEHUB_OBSERVE_KEY / VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL_KEY come from the credential store, never argv.)
#
# ⚠ The per-order NOTIONAL ceiling is NOT an environment variable any more.
# VIKE_TRADEHUB_MAX_ORDER_NOTIONAL was REMOVED (settings unification, Phase 5): a ceiling that a
# stale `Environment=` line in this file can raise is not a ceiling. Setting it here now makes the
# daemon REFUSE TO START, naming its replacement — put it in <project>/settings/policy.toml instead
# (docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md §3c has the line to write).
#
# HFT thread→core pinning (honored by the core builders the daemon reuses).
# Environment=VIKE_PIN_CORES=md:2,core:3,exec:4

# --- WHERE SETTINGS AND CREDENTIALS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------------
# `<project>/settings/` holds policy.toml, config.toml, preferences.toml, flags.toml, secrets.env
# and state/. The binary finds it by walking UP from its working directory for a project marker: a
# checkout's `Cargo.toml` declaring `[workspace]`, else the NEAREST marker of either kind — for a
# DEPLOYMENT, its own `settings/` directory.
#
# ⚠ A DEPLOYED box has a BINARY, not a source checkout. Until the `settings/` marker existed the walk
# returned NOTHING here, and this daemon started with no policy ceiling and NO CREDENTIALS: every
# venue silently on paper, no error in the log, because "no settings" and "settings that say nothing"
# look identical downstream.
#
# So the deployment must have the directory — AND its `state/` sub-directory, which under
# ProtectSystem=strict the daemon cannot create for itself (see the ReadWritePaths block at the
# bottom, which is also why `state` is listed there):
#   install -d -m700 /srv/vike-<unit>/settings /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state
#   install -m600 /path/to/secrets.env /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/secrets.env
#
# The line below then makes the answer EXPLICIT and independent of WorkingDirectory — set it and no
# walk happens at all. Keep it: a unit that resolves its credentials by probing its CWD is one
# `systemd-run --working-directory` away from finding nothing.
Environment=VIKE_SETTINGS_DIR=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings

# Operator tunables (NOT credentials) from a plain env file at the PROJECT ROOT. Harmless if absent —
# the `-` makes it optional.
#
# ⚠ It MUST NOT be settings/secrets.env. The credential store is the one file in this workspace that
# is deliberately NEVER an environment source, and four separate gates are built on that guarantee:
# VIKE_TRADEHUB_LIVE, VIKE_TRADEHUB_CONTROL, VIKE_RECONCILE and VIKE_TELEGRAM_CONTROL are all read
# off the REAL process env (`std::env::vars()`), NOT the credential map, precisely BECAUSE the store
# never reaches there — crates/vike-ops/src/reconcile_config.rs's module doc is the authority on that
# reasoning. EnvironmentFile= at the store inverts it: one line appended to a file every operator is
# told holds credentials would then mount live venues or open the remote control surface, with no
# unit edit and no review. It also exports every venue key into /proc/<pid>/environ and
# `systemctl show -p Environment`, inherited by every child process — the leak the separation exists
# to prevent.
#
# ⚠ Whatever DOES go here still reaches the process environment, where a REMOVED setting is refused
# at startup rather than ignored (crates/vike-config/src/lib.rs's `REMOVED_ENV`).
EnvironmentFile=-/srv/vike-<unit>/.env

# --- keep the daemon from starving a live node ---------------------------------------------------
# prod boxes may also host live trading; cap memory so a runaway can never starve it. Tune to the
# box's headroom.
MemoryMax=2G
# Restart on a crash (an OOM kill included), but not on a clean exit / operator stop.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
# ⚠ Restart=on-failure and a failure NOTIFIER are different things, and this template ships only the
# first. `OnFailure=` fires when the unit enters the FAILED state, which a successful restart does
# not — so a daemon that crashes and recovers restarts forever, silently, visible only as a rising
# NRestarts=. the CI box's installed unit carries the hook inline as `OnFailure=vike-notify@%n.service`;
# deploy/attach-onfailure.sh
# attaches it as a drop-in to a unit whose file you do not own. Do not do both: OnFailure= is a LIST
# directive and you would get two notifications per failure.

# Hardening: the daemon needs no privileges and (paper) no venue network.
NoNewPrivileges=true
# ⚠ ProtectSystem=strict mounts the WHOLE filesystem read-only except /dev, /proc, /sys and
# whatever ReadWritePaths= names. That includes the project root — so `settings/` and everything
# under it is READ-ONLY to this daemon except the one grant below. Reads are unaffected: policy.toml,
# config.toml and secrets.env all load normally, which is exactly why this never showed up in
# testing — the paper daemon reads its settings and writes no state, so the ceiling arms, the venues
# mount, and nothing complains.
#
# ⚠ MEASURED on the CI box (systemd 255, Ubuntu 24.04): under a `systemd --user` manager with the
# distribution default `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`, these four directives are
# applied as NO-OPS — no warning, no failed unit, and `systemctl --user show` still reports them as
# configured. They enforce under the SYSTEM manager. If the sandbox is load-bearing for you, install
# system-wide with `User=`, and verify rather than assume: docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md has the probe.
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=true
# TWO grants, and deliberately the SUB-directories rather than the project root. Granting the root
# would hand the daemon write access to the policy.toml that caps it — a daemon that can rewrite its
# own ceiling has none — and to the binary it is running. Anything writable by this unit must be
# data, never code.
#
# ⚠ The paths must EXIST — `write_path`'s lazy `create_dir_all` cannot make one under a read-only
# parent. Under the SYSTEM manager systemd also refuses to start a unit whose ReadWritePaths= names
# a missing path (status=226), and that refusal is the point: it names the directory, where a silent
# degradation would not. ⚠ Under a --user manager there is NO such refusal — the same root cause as
# the sandbox no-op noted above, measured on the CI box with the probe in docs/ops/tradehub-the CI box.md — so
# create them with the `install -d` lines in the settings block above rather than relying on it.
#
# ⚠ BOTH PATHS ON ONE LINE, and `crates/vike-ops/tests/deploy_layout_gate.rs` refuses a second:
# `ReadWritePaths=` is a LIST directive, so a second LINE APPENDS rather than replaces — the silent
# way a sandbox widens — and the whole writable set belongs where one glance reads it.
#
#   [1] STATE — the rolling log (settings/state/logs) and the daemon's program state
#       (`<settings>/state/alerts.json` today, and the ONE root every later state file lands in,
#       `vike_model::state_path`).
#   [2] THE SETTINGS DATABASE — argued below.
ReadWritePaths=/srv/vike-<unit>/settings/state /srv/vike-<unit>/settings/db
#
# [2] THE SETTINGS DATABASE, the second path above — **added on the owner's ruling of 2026-09-18**
# (*"why doesn't the daemon have access to write to the db? it has to have access"*), after it was
# measured that it did not.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-18 inside the live daemon's own mount namespace (`nsenter -t $MainPID -m`), which
# is the only place the fact is visible:
#
#     REFUSED   <root>/settings        Read-only file system
#     REFUSED   <root>/settings/db     Read-only file system
#     WRITABLE  <root>/settings/state
#
# …while the SAME path from an ordinary shell, as the SAME user, is `drwx------ the operator the operator`
# and writable. The read-only-ness is a property of this unit's mount namespace, not of the
# filesystem, so every error message that sends an operator to `ls -l` sends them where it cannot be
# seen. The store moved to `<settings>/db/vike.db` when the credentials migrated (2026-09-14) and
# this grant did not move with it, so every write the daemon owns there — the account lifecycle and
# the credential upsert behind it — failed at `open_for_write` with a bare SQLite string.
#
# ⚠ **THE CEILING IS IN THIS FILE TOO — so a FILESYSTEM grant is the wrong instrument to guard it
# with, and this block will not pretend otherwise.** `crates/vike-secrets/src/schema.rs` creates
# seven tables, three of them settings: `setting`, `venue_arming` (the per-venue ARMING CEILING) and
# `profile_risk`. Granting the directory grants all seven, because a mount namespace cannot see a
# table.
#
# An earlier draft of this block argued the grant was safe because
# `docs/decisions/0057-the-seven-settings-files-answered-one-at-a-time.md` Phase 1 writes the store
# and lets **the FILES still win** — true today, and proven by
# `crates/vike-config/tests/mirror.rs`'s `the_mirror_changes_no_effective_value`. **That argument was
# refused by the owner on 2026-09-18 and he is right**: 0057 and
# `docs/decisions/0054-settings-move-into-one-database.md` exist to RETIRE those files. An argument
# whose premise the roadmap is built to delete is an argument with an expiry date nobody will be
# watching for — and it protects `policy.toml`, the artefact being abandoned, while handing over the
# place the ceiling is moving TO.
#
# **So the guard belongs in the DATABASE, and it is OWED.** The seam already exists and is narrow:
# every write in that store goes through `crates/vike-secrets/src/db.rs`'s `open_for_write`, and the
# three ceiling tables are written by `crates/vike-secrets/src/settings.rs` ALONE — the daemon's own
# paths (`edit_account_in`, the credential upsert) touch `account` and `credential` and nothing else.
# A `Connection::authorizer` (rusqlite's `hooks` feature, not currently enabled) installed on the
# connection the daemon opens would make the ceiling tables unwritable by this process STRUCTURALLY,
# whatever moves into the file later, and would make this grant permanently safe rather than safe
# until a migration lands. Until that exists, what stands between this daemon and its own ceiling is
# that no code path in it writes those tables — a fact, but not an enforced one.
#
# The fallback, if the authorizer is judged too much: the PARK-AND-FOLD shape
# `crates/vike-model/src/account_confirmation.rs` already uses — the sandboxed daemon writes
# `settings/state`, and an un-sandboxed `vike-cli` verb folds it into the database.
#
# The Telegram at-most-once ledger is COVERED by the grant above. A `--features telegram` build
# persists Telegram's `update_id` high-water mark — the thing that stops a restart replaying an order
# command — and `telegram_ledger_paths` resolves it to `<settings>/state/telegram_updates.ledger`,
# i.e. inside ReadWritePaths= already; no extra grant, and nothing further to do.
#
# It also no longer degrades quietly if the path is wrong: an unreadable or unappendable ledger
# REFUSES TO ARM the channel (one error! naming the cause) instead of trading on with at-most-once
# switched off, and a mark that stops persisting under a running daemon drops that update
# undispatched. So a misconfigured layout here costs the remote-control channel, never a duplicate
# order — the daemon keeps trading headless either way.
#
# ⚠ Still do NOT add the exe directory to ReadWritePaths=. `<exe_dir>/telegram_updates.log` is the
# PRE-MOVE path and is now READ ONLY AT OPEN, purely to carry an existing install's high-water mark
# forward across the move; it is never written, so it needs no grant. A daemon that can rewrite its
# own binary is a worse problem than any it would solve here.
#
# ⚠ The HALT sentinel is the other standing temptation to grant it, and the answer is the same. Its
# built-in default USED to be `<exe_dir>/HALT`, so the obvious "fix" for an EROFS on `touch` was to
# grant the bin directory — which buys one writable sentinel and hands the daemon write access to the
# binary it is running. The default moved to <project>/settings/state/HALT instead, into the grant
# that already exists above; VIKE_HALT_FILE= says the same thing explicitly. The grant stays where it
# is.


# --- syscall / capability hardening, MEASURED ON THE REAL BINARIES -------------------------------
# ⚠ MEASURED on the CI box 2026-08-17, systemd 255, with throwaway SYSTEM units running these exact
# binaries — the set below is not a template copied from a hardening guide, every line survived a
# run. The recorder's own `--once` dry run exits 0 under it, and that run dials 8 live polymarket
# streams and 2 binance streams, so the TLS handshakes and WebSocket dials happen UNDER the seccomp
# filter; vike-tradehub reaches `{"kind":"ready"}`. `systemd-analyze security`: recorder 8.7 -> 1.9,
# tradehub 8.3 -> 1.7. Before this block the units carried the four filesystem directives above and
# NONE of these — the whole syscall and capability surface was open.
#
# Nothing here is a guess about what the daemon needs: it runs as an ordinary user, acquires no
# capability, creates no namespace of its own, takes no realtime priority and executes one ABI.
#
# ⚠ PrivateNetwork= is deliberately ABSENT and always will be — these daemons ARE network clients.
# `systemd-analyze` charges 0.5 for that plus 0.3 for holding AF_INET sockets at all; both are
# unrecoverable for anything that talks to a venue, and adding it to chase the number would produce a
# daemon that cannot do its job. RestrictAddressFamilies= therefore ALLOWS the three families in use
# rather than pretending the internet is optional.
#
# ⚠ The one line with a real blast radius is UMask=0077: new files become owner-only. Checked on
# the CI box before adopting — `lsof +D <project>` shows exactly one user touching the tree, so nothing
# else reads the store today. A future reader under a DIFFERENT account breaks on it, loudly, with a
# permission error rather than silence. That is the intended trade for a tree holding venue keys.
CapabilityBoundingSet=
AmbientCapabilities=
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallFilter=~@privileged @resources @obsolete @mount @module @reboot @swap @raw-io @debug @clock @cpu-emulation
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
UMask=0077
[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
